<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jean-Pierre Boyer</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1776–1850</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jean-Pierre Boyer was president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> from 1818 to 1843. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-christophe">Henri Christophe</a>'s death in 1820 he reunited the north with the republic, and in 1822 he occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a>, ruling all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a> until 1844. In 1825 he accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>'s demand for an indemnity of 150 million francs in return for recognition, a lasting burden. He was overthrown in 1843 and died in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jean-pierre-boyer.jpg" alt="Jean-Pierre Boyer" width="320" height="390" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: B.C. (Name unknown, beyond these initials) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:President_Jean-Pierre_Boyer_of_Haiti_(Hispaniola_Unification_Regime)_Portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
